 samples collected during fieldwork. The data are held in GDA 94","id":"Lime sample analyses","proxyUrl":null,"proxyGetFeatureInfoUrl":null,"proxyCountUrl":null,"proxyStyleUrl":null,"proxyDownloadUrl":null,"mapStyles":null,"feature_count":0,"singleTile":false,"legendImg":null,"previewImg":null,"group":"Soil Chemistry","layerMode":"NA","cswRecords":[{"name":"Lime sample analyses (DMIRS-039)","resourceProvider":"Unknown","id":"4b3c3170-c3cd-4f4d-8954-e774f32ddc69","recordInfoUrl":"https://portal.auscope.org.au/geonetwork/srv/eng/catalog.search#/metadata/4b3c3170-c3cd-4f4d-8954-e774f32ddc69","description":"Lime sample analyses contains geoscientific and analytical data from calcrete, dolomite, limesand and limestone samples collected during fieldwork. The data are held in GDA 94\n\nLicense: Creative Commons Attribution\n\nTags: Analyses, Geology, Geosciences, Industry, Lime, Mine, Minerals, Mining\n\nContact: landuseplanning@dmirs.wa.gov.au","noCache":false,"service":false,"adminArea":null,"contactOrg":"Data WA","contactPerson":null,"funderOrg":"Unknown","authors":[],"onlineResources":[{"url":"https://public-services.slip.wa.gov.au:443/public/services/SLIP_Public_Services/Geology_and_Soils_Map/MapServer/WMSServer?","type":"WMS","name":"26","description":"Lime sample analyses contains geoscientific and analytical data from calcrete, dolomite, limesand and limestone samples collected during fieldwork. The data are held in GDA 94\n\nLicense: Creative Commons Attribution\n\nTags: Analyses, Geology, Geosciences, Industry, Lime, Mine, Minerals, Mining\n\nContact: landuseplanning@dmirs.wa.gov.au","version":"1.1.1","applicationProfile":"","protocolRequest":""}],"geographicElements":[{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":122.8615,"westBoundLongitude":114.0097,"northBoundLatitude":-17.5909,"southBoundLatitude":-35.1113}],"descriptiveKeywords":["SLIP","Soil","World","Earth Sciences"],"datasetURIs":[],"constraints":["","https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0"],"useLimitConstraints":["https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0"],"accessConstraints":[],"childRecords":[],"date":"2026-06-18T00:00:00 UTC","minScale":null,"maxScale":null,"knownLayerIds":["Lime sample analyses"],"knownLayerNames":["WA Lime Sample Analyses"],"knownLayerDescriptions":["Lime sample analyses contains geoscientific and analytical data from calcrete, dolomite, limesand and limestone samples collected during fieldwork. The data are held in GDA 94"]}],"relatedRecords":[],"capabilityRecords":[]},{"name":"GSWA Geochemistry","hidden":false,"description":"The WACHEM database is a compilation of multi-element geochemistry of rocks unconsolidated surface material (regolith) and drill core collected by the Geological Survey of Western Australia.","id":"gswa-geochemistry","proxyUrl":null,"proxyGetFeatureInfoUrl":null,"proxyCountUrl":null,"proxyStyleUrl":null,"proxyDownloadUrl":null,"mapStyles":null,"feature_count":0,"singleTile":false,"legendImg":null,"previewImg":null,"group":"Geochemistry","layerMode":"NA","cswRecords":[{"name":"GSWA Geochemistry (DMIRS-047)","resourceProvider":"Unknown","id":"dfbba304-bd7c-4745-a9de-ecf1b894072e","recordInfoUrl":"https://portal.auscope.org.au/geonetwork/srv/eng/catalog.search#/metadata/dfbba304-bd7c-4745-a9de-ecf1b894072e","description":"The WACHEM database is a compilation of multi-element geochemistry of rocks unconsolidated surface material (regolith) and drill core collected by the Geological Survey of Western Australia. Samples have been analysed for a range of major element oxides, trace elements, rare earth elements (REE), and isotopes by a variety of analytical approaches at commercial, government and university laboratories. In most cases, analysis of unknowns has been carried out along with samples of know composition (reference materials), a second sample of the unknown (sample duplicate), and material which does not contain detectable amounts of elements of interest (blank). These data are included with analysis of unknowns, and these quality control data can be used to gauge the quality of analysis. For inclusion in the geochemical database, analytical data for each element in each sample must include the unit of measurement, lower level of detection (LLD) of that element, and an indication of the analytical technique (usually specified as a laboratory-specific code). To minimise the inclusion of spurious data in the database, analytical batch data are loaded with reference to a set of look up tables, against which data can be checked for consistency. \n\nLicense: Creative Commons Attribution\n\nTags: GSWA, Geochemistry, Geology, Geoscience, Minerals, WACHEM\n\nContact: zzSlipDataAdmin@dmirs.wa.gov.au","noCache":false,"service":false,"adminArea":null,"contactOrg":"Data WA","contactPerson":null,"funderOrg":"Unknown","authors":[],"onlineResources":[{"url":"https://public-services.slip.wa.gov.au:443/public/services/SLIP_Public_Services/Geology_and_Soils_Map/MapServer/WMSServer?","type":"WMS","name":"27","description":"The WACHEM database is a compilation of multi-element geochemistry of rocks unconsolidated surface material (regolith) and drill core collected by the Geological Survey of Western Australia. Samples have been analysed for a range of major element oxides, trace elements, rare earth elements (REE), and isotopes by a variety of analytical approaches at commercial, government and university laboratories. In most cases, analysis of unknowns has been carried out along with samples of know composition (reference materials), a second sample of the unknown (sample duplicate), and material which does not contain detectable amounts of elements of interest (blank). These data are included with analysis of unknowns, and these quality control data can be used to gauge the quality of analysis. For inclusion in the geochemical database, analytical data for each element in each sample must include the unit of measurement, lower level of detection (LLD) of that element, and an indication of the analytical technique (usually specified as a laboratory-specific code). To minimise the inclusion of spurious data in the database, analytical batch data are loaded with reference to a set of look up tables, against which data can be checked for consistency. \n\nLicense: Creative Commons Attribution\n\nTags: GSWA, Geochemistry, Geology, Geoscience, Minerals, WACHEM\n\nContact: zzSlipDataAdmin@dmirs.wa.gov.au","version":"1.1.1","applicationProfile":"","protocolRequest":""}],"geographicElements":[{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":129.0257,"westBoundLongitude":111.5896,"northBoundLatitude":-13.7777,"southBoundLatitude":-35.0635}],"descriptiveKeywords":["SLIP","Soil","World","Earth Sciences"],"datasetURIs":[],"constraints":["","https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0"],"useLimitConstraints":["https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0"],"accessConstraints":[],"childRecords":[],"date":"2026-06-18T00:00:00 UTC","minScale":null,"maxScale":null,"knownLayerIds":["gswa-geochemistry"],"knownLayerNames":["GSWA Geochemistry"],"knownLayerDescriptions":["The WACHEM database is a compilation of multi-element geochemistry of rocks unconsolidated surface material (regolith) and drill core collected by the Geological Survey of Western Australia."]}],"relatedRecords":[],"capabilityRecords":[]},{"name":"GSWA Geochronology","hidden":false,"description":"Isotopic age determinations (U–Pb, Ar–Ar, and K–Ar mineral and whole-rock methods) obtained from geological samples throughout Western Australia.","id":"GSWA Geochronology","proxyUrl":null,"proxyGetFeatureInfoUrl":null,"proxyCountUrl":null,"proxyStyleUrl":null,"proxyDownloadUrl":null,"mapStyles":null,"feature_count":0,"singleTile":false,"legendImg":null,"previewImg":null,"group":"Geochronology","layerMode":"NA","cswRecords":[{"name":"GSWA Geochronology (DMIRS-029)","resourceProvider":"Unknown","id":"72f551ed-7ab4-409f-a126-6581eb3350bb","recordInfoUrl":"https://portal.auscope.org.au/geonetwork/srv/eng/catalog.search#/metadata/72f551ed-7ab4-409f-a126-6581eb3350bb","description":"Isotopic age determinations (U–Pb, Ar–Ar, and K–Ar mineral and whole-rock methods) obtained from geological samples throughout Western Australia.\n\nLicense: Creative Commons Attribution\n\nTags: GSWA, Geochronology, Geology, Geoscience, Industry, Isotopic, Minerals\n\nContact: geological.survey@dmirs.wa.gov.au","noCache":false,"service":false,"adminArea":null,"contactOrg":"Data WA","contactPerson":null,"funderOrg":"Unknown","authors":[],"onlineResources":[{"url":"https://public-services.slip.wa.gov.au:443/public/services/SLIP_Public_Services/Geology_and_Soils_Map/MapServer/WMSServer?","type":"WMS","name":"28","description":"Isotopic age determinations (U–Pb, Ar–Ar, and K–Ar mineral and whole-rock methods) obtained from geological samples throughout Western Australia.\n\nLicense: Creative Commons Attribution\n\nTags: GSWA, Geochronology, Geology, Geoscience, Industry, Isotopic, Minerals\n\nContact: geological.survey@dmirs.wa.gov.au","version":"1.1.1","applicationProfile":"","protocolRequest":""}],"geographicElements":[{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":128.9905,"westBoundLongitude":110.2495,"northBoundLatitude":-14.0594,"southBoundLatitude":-35.0635}],"descriptiveKeywords":["SLIP","Soil","World","Earth Sciences"],"datasetURIs":[],"constraints":["","https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0"],"useLimitConstraints":["https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0"],"accessConstraints":[],"childRecords":[],"date":"2026-06-18T00:00:00 UTC","minScale":null,"maxScale":null,"knownLayerIds":["GSWA Geochronology"],"knownLayerNames":["GSWA Geochronology"],"knownLayerDescriptions":["Isotopic age determinations (U–Pb, Ar–Ar, and K–Ar mineral and whole-rock methods) obtained from geological samples throughout Western Australia."]}],"relatedRecords":[],"capabilityRecords":[]},{"name":"ALIRT-001 Level 1 Decimated Data","hidden":false,"description":"Distributed Acoustic Sensing (DAS) provides a new, non-invasive mean for high resolution subsurface imaging in urban, developed environments by repurposing existing telecommunication cables. This DAS dataset is collected over 3 months between December 2021 and April 2022 at a fibre optic network dedicated to research, i.e., ALIRT (Australian Lightwave Infrastructure Research Testbed) in Melbourne, Australia. In this deployment, a 25 km long fibre optic cable is transformed into 6252 channels at 4 m spacing, starting from Melbourne Central Business District running west to east. Silixa iDAS interrogator is used, which measures strain-rate at 1 kHz with a fixed gauge length of 10 m. The time series recorded is decimated to 62.5 Hz. This dataset represents the ALIRT-001 Level 1 Decimated Data outputs.","id":"alirt-level-01-decimated-data","proxyUrl":null,"proxyGetFeatureInfoUrl":null,"proxyCountUrl":null,"proxyStyleUrl":null,"proxyDownloadUrl":null,"mapStyles":null,"feature_count":0,"singleTile":false,"legendImg":null,"previewImg":null,"group":"Distributed Acoustic Sensing","geojson":{"bbox":[[144.8,-37.9],[145.3,-37.7]]},"layerMode":"NA","cswRecords":[{"name":"Australian Lightwave Infrastructure Research Testbed Dataset (ALIRT-001) - Level 1 Decimated Data","resourceProvider":"Unknown","id":"f7951_4499_3694_3652","recordInfoUrl":"https://geonetwork.nci.org.au/geonetwork/srv/eng/catalog.search#/metadata/f7951_4499_3694_3652","description":"Distributed Acoustic Sensing (DAS) provides a non-invasive means for high-resolution subsurface imaging by repurposing existing fibre-optic telecommunication infrastructure. This dataset was collected using the Australian Lightwave Infrastructure Research Testbed (ALIRT), a dedicated research fibre network in Melbourne, Australia, where a 25-km-long fibre-optic cable was transformed into a DAS array. Data were acquired between December 2021 and April 2022, spanning from the Melbourne Central Business District toward the eastern suburbs.\r\nThe fibre was interrogated using a Silixa iDAS interrogator enabled by AuScope, recording strain-rate at 1 kHz with a fixed gauge length of 10 m and a channel spacing of 4 m. A SmartSolo nodal seismic array (FDSN network code: 4Z) was deployed concurrently across the Melbourne metropolitan area.\r\nLevel-1 DAS data were temporally decimated from 1,000 Hz to 62.5 Hz, converted from raw integer counts to strain-rate, and stored in HDF5 format. No spatial decimation was applied.","noCache":false,"service":false,"adminArea":null,"contactOrg":"Research School of Earth Sciences (RSES), Australian National University","contactPerson":null,"funderOrg":"Unknown","authors":["Lai, Voon Hui","Miller, Meghan","Lai, Voon Hui","Miller, Meghan","Lai, Voon Hui","Miller, Meghan","Lai, Voon Hui","Miller, Meghan"],"onlineResources":[],"geographicElements":[{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":145.145,"westBoundLongitude":144.96,"northBoundLatitude":-37.8,"southBoundLatitude":-37.85}],"descriptiveKeywords":["geophysics, seismology, DAS, passive seismic, earthquake, subsurface, fibre-optic cables",""],"datasetURIs":["10.25914/1phc-8r73"],"constraints":["https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","Access to this collection will be restricted until the end of 2025.","RestrictionCode: license","Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","RestrictionCode: otherRestrictions"],"useLimitConstraints":["Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","RestrictionCode: license","RestrictionCode: otherRestrictions"],"accessConstraints":["RestrictionCode: license"],"childRecords":[],"date":"2023-03-14T00:00:00 UTC","minScale":null,"maxScale":null,"knownLayerIds":["alirt-level-01-decimated-data"],"knownLayerNames":["ALIRT-001 Level 1 Decimated Data"],"knownLayerDescriptions":["Distributed Acoustic Sensing (DAS) provides a new, non-invasive mean for high resolution subsurface imaging in urban, developed environments by repurposing existing telecommunication cables. This DAS dataset is collected over 3 months between December 2021 and April 2022 at a fibre optic network dedicated to research, i.e., ALIRT (Australian Lightwave Infrastructure Research Testbed) in Melbourne, Australia. In this deployment, a 25 km long fibre optic cable is transformed into 6252 channels at 4 m spacing, starting from Melbourne Central Business District running west to east. Silixa iDAS interrogator is used, which measures strain-rate at 1 kHz with a fixed gauge length of 10 m. The time series recorded is decimated to 62.5 Hz. This dataset represents the ALIRT-001 Level 1 Decimated Data outputs."]}],"relatedRecords":[],"capabilityRecords":[]},{"name":"Cloncurry METAL: Integrated Dataset","hidden":false,"description":"Cloncurry Multi Element Toolkit and Laboratory (METAL) aims to shift the “Big Data” paradigm in mineral system science by developing a quantitative, fully integrated, multi-modal, scale-consistent methodology for system characterisation. The data comprises collocated petrophysical-mineralogical-geochemical-structural-metasomatic characterisation of 23 deposits from a highly complex mineral system. This approach allows translation of mineral system processes into physics, providing a framework for smarter geophysics-based exploration.","id":"cloncurry-metal","proxyUrl":null,"proxyGetFeatureInfoUrl":null,"proxyCountUrl":null,"proxyStyleUrl":null,"proxyDownloadUrl":null,"mapStyles":null,"feature_count":0,"singleTile":false,"legendImg":null,"previewImg":null,"group":"Integrated Geoscience Dataset","layerMode":"NA","cswRecords":[{"name":"Cloncurry METAL: multimodal integrated mineral system characterisation data","resourceProvider":"Unknown","id":"eda39b66-11ed-490b-9aa6-21f5653bf27f","recordInfoUrl":"https://portal.auscope.org.au/geonetwork/srv/eng/catalog.search#/metadata/eda39b66-11ed-490b-9aa6-21f5653bf27f","description":"Cloncurry Multi Element Toolkit and Laboratory (METAL) aims to shift the “Big Data” paradigm in mineral system science by developing a quantitative, fully integrated, multi-modal, scale-consistent methodology for system characterisation. The data comprises collocated petrophysical-mineralogical-geochemical-structural-metasomatic characterisation of 23 deposits from a highly complex mineral system. This approach allows translation of mineral system processes into physics, providing a framework for smarter geophysics-based exploration.","noCache":false,"service":false,"adminArea":null,"contactOrg":"AuScope Data Repository","contactPerson":null,"funderOrg":"Unknown","authors":["James R. Austin"],"onlineResources":[],"geographicElements":[{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":141.65563617914438,"westBoundLongitude":139.58982579007,"northBoundLatitude":-18.506958939415476,"southBoundLatitude":-22.539195831421416}],"descriptiveKeywords":["BHT","Exploration","IOCG","ISCG","SEM mineralogy","cloncurry","copper","data integration","geophysics","gold","gravity","integration by design","magnetics","mount isa","petrophysics","queensland","spectral mineralogy","AuScope Data Repository"],"datasetURIs":["https://doi.org/10.60623/82trleue"],"constraints":[],"useLimitConstraints":[],"accessConstraints":[],"childRecords":[],"date":"2025-06-19T00:00:00 UTC","minScale":null,"maxScale":null,"knownLayerIds":["cloncurry-metal"],"knownLayerNames":["Cloncurry METAL: Integrated Dataset"],"knownLayerDescriptions":["Cloncurry Multi Element Toolkit and Laboratory (METAL) aims to shift the “Big Data” paradigm in mineral system science by developing a quantitative, fully integrated, multi-modal, scale-consistent methodology for system characterisation. The data comprises collocated petrophysical-mineralogical-geochemical-structural-metasomatic characterisation of 23 deposits from a highly complex mineral system. This approach allows translation of mineral system processes into physics, providing a framework for smarter geophysics-based exploration."]}],"relatedRecords":[],"capabilityRecords":[]},{"name":"Calperum Observatory Heat Needle Monitoring Program","hidden":false,"description":"The dataset is ground temperature and thermal conductivity data recorded at the Calperum Critical Zone observatory with AuScope Heat Needle devices.","id":"calperum-heat-needle","proxyUrl":null,"proxyGetFeatureInfoUrl":null,"proxyCountUrl":null,"proxyStyleUrl":null,"proxyDownloadUrl":null,"mapStyles":null,"feature_count":0,"singleTile":false,"legendImg":null,"previewImg":null,"group":"Temperature","layerMode":"NA","cswRecords":[{"name":"Calperum Critical Zone Observatory (CZO) Heat Needle Monitoring Program Feb 2024-ongoing","resourceProvider":"Unknown","id":"d73d2881-e518-4311-8373-423b5bc8d7ae","recordInfoUrl":"https://portal.auscope.org.au/geonetwork/srv/eng/catalog.search#/metadata/d73d2881-e518-4311-8373-423b5bc8d7ae","description":"The dataset is ground temperature and thermal conductivity data recorded at the Calperum Critical Zone observatory with AuScope Heat Needle devices. \n\nIt comprises two file types. TLOG files record ground temperature at millikelvin resolution at seven depths from surface to 1.1 m continuously at 15-minute intervals. TCLOG files record temperatures during periodic one-hour heating tests for the purpose of thermal conductivity measurements. \n\nData Releases - The data is accumulated over time in newer releases. Each subsequent release will include new data appended to all prior data from previous releases, with no changes to the previous data. A “readme.txt” file is provided in the dataset, which explains the structure and content of the dataset. It includes details about using metadata and generating processed files.","noCache":false,"service":false,"adminArea":null,"contactOrg":"AuScope Data Repository","contactPerson":null,"funderOrg":"Unknown","authors":["Graeme Beardsmore"],"onlineResources":[],"geographicElements":[{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":140.587723,"westBoundLongitude":140.587723,"northBoundLatitude":-34.002355,"southBoundLatitude":-34.002355},{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":140.587511,"westBoundLongitude":140.587511,"northBoundLatitude":-34.002461,"southBoundLatitude":-34.002461},{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":140.58776,"westBoundLongitude":140.58776,"northBoundLatitude":-34.002525,"southBoundLatitude":-34.002525}],"descriptiveKeywords":["Heat Flow","Heat Needle","Land Surface Temperature","Soil Temperature","Thermal Conductivity","Thermal Diffusivity","AuScope Data Repository"],"datasetURIs":["https://doi.org/10.60623/3159pc3b"],"constraints":[],"useLimitConstraints":[],"accessConstraints":[],"childRecords":[],"date":"2025-06-19T00:00:00 UTC","minScale":null,"maxScale":null,"knownLayerIds":["calperum-heat-needle"],"knownLayerNames":["Calperum Observatory Heat Needle Monitoring Program"],"knownLayerDescriptions":["The dataset is ground temperature and thermal conductivity data recorded at the Calperum Critical Zone observatory with AuScope Heat Needle devices."]}],"relatedRecords":[],"capabilityRecords":[]},{"name":"Gippsland and Murray Basin Fiber Optic DTS Study","hidden":false,"description":"This dataset contains both raw and processed data files. The data consists of temperature-depth profiles collected from boreholes using an AP Sensing N4386B and Silixa XT-DTS Distributed Temperature Sensing (DTS) instruments. The purpose of this data collection was to conduct a comparative study between DTS and conventional thermistor-based wireline logging methods.","id":"gippsland-murray-basin-dts-study","proxyUrl":null,"proxyGetFeatureInfoUrl":null,"proxyCountUrl":null,"proxyStyleUrl":null,"proxyDownloadUrl":null,"mapStyles":null,"feature_count":0,"singleTile":false,"legendImg":null,"previewImg":null,"group":"Temperature","layerMode":"NA","cswRecords":[{"name":"Gippsland and Murray Basin Fiber Optic Distributed Temperature Sensing Data","resourceProvider":"Unknown","id":"3779805a-a887-4e98-ac0a-3aea733d5a87","recordInfoUrl":"https://portal.auscope.org.au/geonetwork/srv/eng/catalog.search#/metadata/3779805a-a887-4e98-ac0a-3aea733d5a87","description":"This dataset contains both raw and processed data files.  \n\nThe data consists of temperature-depth profiles collected from boreholes using an AP Sensing N4386B and Silixa XT-DTS Distributed Temperature Sensing (DTS) instruments.  \n\nThe purpose of this data collection was to conduct a comparative study between DTS and conventional thermistor-based wireline logging methods. This comparison aimed to evaluate if DTS could serve as a viable alternative or complementary approach to conventional techniques in measuring borehole temperatures.  \n\nThe study was conducted as part of a master’s project, focusing on maximising the amount of data captured with DTS to compare its precision, accuracy, efficiency, and ease of deployment compared to the conventional method.","noCache":false,"service":false,"adminArea":null,"contactOrg":"AuScope Data Repository","contactPerson":null,"funderOrg":"Unknown","authors":["Graeme Beardsmore"],"onlineResources":[],"geographicElements":[{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":147.467546,"westBoundLongitude":139.8738474,"northBoundLatitude":-34.7661707,"southBoundLatitude":-38.530582}],"descriptiveKeywords":["Borehole","DTS","Distributed Temperature Sensing","Fiber Optics","Gippsland Basin","Murray Basin","AuScope Data Repository"],"datasetURIs":["https://doi.org/10.60623/l8hc57ty"],"constraints":[],"useLimitConstraints":[],"accessConstraints":[],"childRecords":[],"date":"2025-06-19T00:00:00 UTC","minScale":null,"maxScale":null,"knownLayerIds":["gippsland-murray-basin-dts-study"],"knownLayerNames":["Gippsland and Murray Basin Fiber Optic DTS Study"],"knownLayerDescriptions":["This dataset contains both raw and processed data files. The data consists of temperature-depth profiles collected from boreholes using an AP Sensing N4386B and Silixa XT-DTS Distributed Temperature Sensing (DTS) instruments. The purpose of this data collection was to conduct a comparative study between DTS and conventional thermistor-based wireline logging methods."]}],"relatedRecords":[],"capabilityRecords":[]},{"name":"UNSW Raw Groundwater Levels","hidden":false,"description":"The Groundwater Infrastructure Program aims to enable long-term groundwater resource research in a variable and changing climate to inform sustainable groundwater management. This dataset contains the groundwater levels and temperature (plus corresponding manual dip measurements) across selected NCRIS sites in New South Wales collected by the Groundwater Research at UNSW","id":"unsw-groundwater-levels","proxyUrl":null,"proxyGetFeatureInfoUrl":null,"proxyCountUrl":null,"proxyStyleUrl":null,"proxyDownloadUrl":null,"mapStyles":null,"feature_count":0,"singleTile":false,"legendImg":null,"previewImg":null,"group":"Groundwater","layerMode":"NA","cswRecords":[{"name":"University of New South Wales (UNSW) Raw Groundwater Levels Data  (2012-2024)","resourceProvider":"Unknown","id":"70e0b9ef-a26a-4eaf-bf0e-6777556279f2","recordInfoUrl":"https://portal.auscope.org.au/geonetwork/srv/eng/catalog.search#/metadata/70e0b9ef-a26a-4eaf-bf0e-6777556279f2","description":"The Groundwater Infrastructure Program aims to enable long-term groundwater resource research in a variable and changing climate to inform sustainable groundwater management. This dataset contains the groundwater levels and temperature (plus corresponding manual dip measurements) across selected NCRIS sites in New South Wales collected by the Groundwater Research at UNSW:\n\n* The Namoi valley infrastructure has been established to develop an improved understanding of groundwater interaction with the Namoi, and Cockburn Rivers, and Maules Creek. A profile of double piezometers has been installed on the banks of the Namoi, close to the Maules Creek confluence. Maules Creek has both losing and gaining reaches and infrastructure (including two weather stations) has been installed in this part of the catchment and to provide permanent monitoring of the system. The Upper part of Maules Creek has been instrumented to estimate groundwater recharge from ephemeral streams.\n\n* The Wellington site focuses on fractured rock aquifers. Fractured and cavernous limestone at the Wellington Caves has a combination of boreholes, a weather station and instrumented caves, uniquely using caves as an observatory of vadose zone processes.  Fractured granites at Baldry have surface water, ground water, climate and tree water use all instrumented. Fractured Ordovician volcanics and metasediments at the UNSW Wellington Research Station have a borefield of over 40 bores in both alluvial sediments and the adjacent fractured rock aquifer. Abstraction bores in both aquifers facilitate experiments such as aquifer tests (pumping tests) and tracer experiments.","noCache":false,"service":false,"adminArea":null,"contactOrg":"AuScope Data Repository","contactPerson":null,"funderOrg":"Unknown","authors":["Martin Andersen"],"onlineResources":[],"geographicElements":[{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":150.0988593,"westBoundLongitude":150.0988593,"northBoundLatitude":-30.48890703,"southBoundLatitude":-30.48890703},{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":150.122327,"westBoundLongitude":150.122327,"northBoundLatitude":-30.49253463,"southBoundLatitude":-30.49253463},{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":149.9620541,"westBoundLongitude":149.9620541,"northBoundLatitude":-30.49392496,"southBoundLatitude":-30.49392496},{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":150.0357901,"westBoundLongitude":150.0357901,"northBoundLatitude":-30.42047161,"southBoundLatitude":-30.42047161},{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":150.1381136,"westBoundLongitude":150.1381136,"northBoundLatitude":-30.48549192,"southBoundLatitude":-30.48549192},{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":150.1628822,"westBoundLongitude":150.1628822,"northBoundLatitude":-30.46586204,"southBoundLatitude":-30.46586204},{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":150.1624323,"westBoundLongitude":150.1624323,"northBoundLatitude":-30.46561962,"southBoundLatitude":-30.46561962},{"type":"bbox","eastBoundLongitude":150.174936,"westBoundLongitude":1